  "saint": {
    "name": "St. Sergius of Radonezh",
    "slug": "sergius-of-radonezh",
    "summary": "A plowman walked a long way to see the famous hegumen of a forest monastery near Radonezh, and found a man in a patched robe digging a garden. \"I came to see a prophet,\" he said, \"and you show me an orphan.\" Then a prince rode in and bowed to the gardener. Sergius of Radonezh was a boy who could not learn to read, a hermit who shared his last crust with a bear, and the monk whose blessing a grand prince sought before he marched against the Horde. How did he become the Hegumen of the Russian land without ever ceasing to be poor?",
    "biography": "<h2 id=\"a-prophet-or-an-orphan\">A Prophet or an Orphan?</h2>\n\n<p>The plowman had come a long way to see the hegumen of the monastery in the forest of Radonezh. The man they showed him was working in the garden. Through a gap in the fence the visitor saw someone in a patched robe of coarse, undyed cloth, digging the earth like any laborer, and he refused to believe that this was the hegumen.</p>\n\n<p>“I came to see a prophet,” he said, “and you show me an orphan.”</p>\n\n<p>The brothers were ready to throw him out. Their hegumen stopped them: the man had come to see him, he told them, and not them. Then Sergius did something the peasant never expected. He bowed to the ground before him, kissed him, sat him at his own right hand, and fed him. Then a prince arrived at the monastery with his armed men. The prince’s guards pushed the peasant out of the way, and the prince bowed before the man in the patched robe. Only then did the visitor understand whom he had called an orphan, and he began to tremble.</p>\n\n<p>Both of them were right. The man in the garden was the one Russians would come to call the Hegumen of the Russian land. A grand prince would ask his blessing before he marched against the Horde. A metropolitan would offer him the leadership of the Russian Church, and his disciples would carry his way of life into one new monastery after another. He was also, by his own choice, as poor as any orphan in the country. He wore cloth that other monks had refused, earned his bread with an axe when the bread ran out, and grew angry when people tried to put his name on a spring.</p>\n\n<p>How did a boy who could not learn to read, who wanted nothing more than a hut in the forest and God, become the father whom princes bowed to? And how did he stay the man digging in the garden to the very end? His biographer, the monk Epiphanius, who spent twenty years gathering what people remembered of him, begins the answer before Sergius was even born, on a Sunday, at the Liturgy, in the Rostov country.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"the-boy-who-could-not-read\">The Boy Who Could Not Read</h2>\n\n<p>Kirill and Maria belonged to a boyar family of the Rostov principality, the landed nobility of old Russia. One Sunday, while Maria was carrying her child, she stood in church for the Divine Liturgy, and three times the child in her womb cried out loud enough for the people around her to hear. The first cry came at the reading of the Gospel, the second at the Cherubic Hymn, and the third at the moment the priest proclaims, “Holy Things for the Holy.” The women around Maria turned to her and asked the question that runs under the rest of this story: “What will this child be?”</p>\n\n<p>Epiphanius knew how such a story sounds. He set it beside the signs told before the births of Ephrem the Syrian and Alypius, as if to say that God had done this before. The boy was baptized Bartholomew. Even the year of his birth is uncertain: one line of the tradition places it in 1314, and another in 1322. The Life tells a stranger detail with more confidence. As a baby, Bartholomew would not take his mother’s milk on Wednesdays and Fridays, the fast days of the Church.</p>\n\n<p>At seven he was sent to school with his brother, and there the child of the three cries became an ordinary failure. He could not learn his letters. His teacher, in despair, beat him, and the boy wept where no one could see him.</p>\n\n<p>One day his father sent him out to look for some foals that had strayed. Epiphanius saw a biblical echo here: young Saul also went out after his father’s lost animals, and met the prophet Samuel on the way. In a field, under an oak, Bartholomew found a monk in a black robe, a priest, standing in prayer with tears. The boy did not interrupt. He waited until the old man had finished, and then he poured out his trouble: he could not learn to read, and he asked the elder to pray for him.</p>\n\n<p>The elder prayed. Then he held out a small piece of white bread, a prosphora, the blessed loaf of the Liturgy. “Open your mouth, child,” he said, “take this and eat.” Bartholomew ate it, and it tasted sweet as honey.</p>\n\n<p>The boy would not let him go. He begged the elder to come home with him, and there the old man told him to read from the Psalter in the chapel. The boy who could not read opened the book and read clearly. Before he left, the elder spoke to Kirill and Maria about their son.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Your son will be a dwelling of the Holy Trinity and will lead many after him to understand the divine commandments.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— The elder to Kirill and Maria, in the Life of Sergius by Epiphanius the Wise, chapter 3 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>At the gate, as he was leaving, the elder vanished. Kirill and Maria decided that their guest had been an angel. Neither of them could know what “a dwelling of the Holy Trinity” meant, and the words would wait many years for their answer.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-boyar-hanged-in-rostov\">A Boyar Hanged in Rostov</h2>\n\n<p>The boy who had learned to read so suddenly began to fast with a seriousness that alarmed his mother. Before he was twelve, he lived on bread and water, and on Wednesdays and Fridays he ate nothing at all. Maria begged him to stop. He answered her from the Scriptures, quoting Job and the fiftieth Psalm: no one is clean before God. His mother’s reply has the ring of any parent faced with a solemn child. “You are not twelve years old,” she said, “and you speak of your sins.”</p>\n\n<p>He was a serious boy, and Epiphanius does not pretend otherwise. Bartholomew did not allow himself laughter, the biographer says, and when he wanted to smile, “he did it with great modesty.”</p>\n\n<p>Around him, the family’s world was coming apart. Kirill had been a rich boyar, but the tribute owed to the Horde, the raids of the Tatars, and one raid in particular, the one led by Turalyk, stripped him of his wealth. Then Rostov itself passed under the power of Moscow. The Muscovite governors, Vasily Kocheva and Mina, came to the town and oppressed it, and the elder boyar of Rostov, a man named Averky, was hanged upside down. The English lives of Sergius usually leave this violence out. Epiphanius did not.</p>\n\n<p>Kirill gathered his family and left. They moved to Radonezh, a small town that Grand Prince Ivan had given to his son Andrei, and settled near the Church of the Nativity of Christ.</p>\n\n<p>Bartholomew already wanted to become a monk. His parents asked him to wait, and he did. He stayed and looked after them until they themselves took the monastic habit, and some years later both of them died. Bartholomew buried them and kept the forty days of prayers for the dead. Then he gave everything he had inherited to his younger brother Petr. His elder brother Stefan had married, lost his wife, and become a monk at the monastery of Khotkovo. It was Stefan who went with Bartholomew to look for a place in the wilderness.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"logs-on-their-shoulders\">Logs on Their Shoulders</h2>\n\n<p>The brothers walked into the forests around Radonezh looking for a deserted place. They found it, the Life says, “in the thicket where there was water.” There they cut down trees and carried the logs on their own shoulders. They built a hut, then a cell, and then a small wooden church.</p>\n\n<p>When they came to the question of whose name the church should bear, it was Stefan who answered. It should be dedicated to the Holy Trinity, he said, because of the three cries his brother had given before he was born. Without either of them saying so, the first part of the elder’s prophecy had come true. The boy who was to be a dwelling of the Holy Trinity now lived beside a church of the Holy Trinity, in a forest no one else wanted. Priests sent by Metropolitan Theognostus came to consecrate it, early in the reign of Grand Prince Simeon.</p>\n\n<p>Stefan did not last. The wilderness was too hard for him, and after a short time he left and went to Moscow, to the Epiphany monastery, where he came to know a monk who would later become Metropolitan Alexis. That friendship would one day matter to his brother. For now, Bartholomew was alone in the forest.</p>\n\n<p>He was tonsured a monk by a hegumen named Mitrofan, on 7 October, the day the Church remembers the martyrs Sergius and Bacchus, and he received the name of the day’s saint. Bartholomew was twenty-three years old. As the deer longs for the stream, he had told Mitrofan, so his soul longed for the monastic life in the desert. When the new monk Sergius received Communion, the little church filled with fragrance. He stayed in the church for seven days afterward, eating nothing but the blessed bread the hegumen had given him.</p>\n\n<p>After those seven days, Sergius faced his nights alone.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-bear-at-the-stump\">A Bear at the Stump</h2>\n\n<p>They were not quiet nights. Once, while he was singing Matins in the empty church, the wall of the church opened, and demons rushed in dressed as Lithuanians, in Lithuanian clothes and tall caps, threatening him. Sergius answered with the Psalms and the sign of the cross, and they were gone. Some days later they came at him again, this time in his hut, and again he drove them off.</p>\n\n<p>The real animals of the forest were nearly as frightening. Wolves howled around his hut, and bears passed by. Epiphanius does not make his hero fearless. Sergius “feared a little, like any man,” the Life says, and he prayed.</p>\n\n<p>One bear came for a different reason. It did not want to hurt him; it was hungry. Sergius began to set out a piece of bread for it on a stump or a log, and the bear came back, again and again. When there was no bread on the stump, the animal would not leave. It stood there for a long time, Epiphanius writes, “like a harsh creditor wanting his debt.” Some days Sergius had only one piece of bread for himself. On those days he broke it in two and gave the bear half. He would go hungry himself, the Life says, rather than cheat the bear. This went on for more than a year.</p>\n\n<p>The Life even keeps the old word for the animal, <em>arkuda</em>. The young hermit was sharing his last crust with a wild beast, and before long men would come to his forest who also needed to be fed.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-knock-on-the-cell-window\">A Knock on the Cell Window</h2>\n\n<p>How long Sergius lived alone, no one knew for certain. Two years or more, Epiphanius guesses, and then adds, with a biographer’s honesty, “I do not know, God knows.” Then the monks began to come, first one, then two, then three. Sergius did not make the life sound attractive. He warned them that they would find hunger, thirst, and poverty here, and that they should prepare their hearts for patience and many trials, and not for food, drink, or rest. They told him they could bear it. Each newcomer built his own cell, and the hermitage began to turn into a village of huts.</p>\n\n<p>Sergius was made a priest and the hegumen of the little community. For two or three years there were twelve brothers and Sergius was the thirteenth, and the number stayed at twelve, as if the forest were keeping count. One of the new arrivals was Symon, an archimandrite from Smolensk. Stefan came too, bringing his young son Ivan, whom he gave to his brother to be made a monk. Sergius tonsured the boy and named him Theodore, and the boy would later be known as Theodore of Simonov.</p>\n\n<p>The hegumen led by doing the hardest work first. He was the first into church for every service, and he stood through the long prayers without leaning on the wall. A man who wanted to join wore a long black coat for a long time and did every kind of labor before he was given the monastic habit.</p>\n\n<p>At night Sergius walked past the cells. Where he heard a brother praying, he blessed him. Where he heard talking or laughter, he knocked on the door or the window, and then walked on. The next morning he would call the brothers who had been talking and speak to them, as Epiphanius puts it, “from afar,” and without anger. Soon the monastery would face worse trouble than late-night talk.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"moldy-bread-and-fresh-loaves\">Moldy Bread and Fresh Loaves</h2>\n\n<p>The monastery ran out of bread, and then out of salt, and the brothers began to go hungry. Sergius had forbidden the monks to go out begging, and he did not lift the rule. He waited three days.</p>\n\n<p>On the fourth morning he picked up an axe and went to the cell of an old monk named Daniil. He offered to build a porch onto Daniil’s cell, and asked to be paid in bread. All day the hegumen of the monastery worked on the old monk’s porch. In the evening Daniil paid him with a sieve full of bread, old bread, already moldy. Sergius sat down and ate it with water.</p>\n\n<p>That was too much for some of the brothers. One monk grumbled out loud that their bread had gone moldy. Others said they would leave the monastery. Sergius was still speaking to them when someone knocked at the gate. Men stood outside with loaves of bread, fresh and fragrant. No one ever learned who had sent them. Hungry as the brothers were, Sergius told them to feed the men who had carried the bread first.</p>\n\n<p>He dressed the same way he ate. His robe was sermyaga, the coarse undyed cloth of peasants. Once a length of cloth came to the monastery that was so poor that seven monks refused it. Sergius alone took it, and he wore the robe made from it until it fell apart.</p>\n\n<p>Robes like that one were what the plowman saw through the gap in the fence. The patched clothing that made Sergius look like an orphan was the clothing he had chosen. Poverty he could choose for himself. Water for a growing community was another matter.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"do-not-call-it-my-spring\">Do Not Call It My Spring</h2>\n\n<p>As more monks arrived, the monastery outgrew its water, and the brothers grumbled at their hegumen again. Why had he settled them in a place with no water? Sergius went down into a ravine, where a little rainwater had collected in a puddle, and prayed there. A spring rose from the ground. Epiphanius says it was still in use when he wrote.</p>\n\n<p>People soon began to call it Sergius’s spring. When he heard it, Sergius was angry. He forbade them ever to call it by his name, because he had not given them the water. The Lord had given it, he said, to all of them, unworthy as they were, himself included.</p>\n\n<p>He was even more careful with a greater wonder. A man came to the monastery carrying his sick little son, and the child died in his arms. The father went away to get a coffin. While he was gone, Sergius knelt and prayed, and the child lived. When the father returned, Sergius would not let him think there had been a miracle. The boy had only been cold, he said, and had warmed up in the cell. No one rises from the dead before the general resurrection. And he forbade the father to tell anyone.</p>\n\n<p>The father could not keep quiet. And one day the monastery in the forest received a letter from much farther away.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"one-rule-is-missing\">One Rule Is Missing</h2>\n\n<p>Greek envoys arrived from Constantinople, from Patriarch Philotheus himself. They brought Sergius gifts: a cross, a paraman, the cloth a monk wears over his shoulders, and the schema, the habit of the highest monastic rank. They also brought a letter, and in it the Patriarch told the hegumen, “One rule is missing: you have no common life.”</p>\n\n<p>Until then each monk had lived in his own cell and kept his own things. The Patriarch wanted something more demanding: a single household in which nothing belonged to anyone. Metropolitan Alexis, the monk Stefan had known in Moscow, read the Patriarch’s letter and agreed. So Sergius gave his monastery a common rule. There were now a cellarer, cooks, bakers, and an infirmarian for the sick, and no brother owned anything of his own. The Life does not say in what year this happened.</p>\n\n<p>From then on, the forest sent out monks as well as receiving them. Sergius founded or helped to found monasteries at Kirzhach, at Simonov, at Dubenka, at Golutvin, and at Vysoky, and he had a part in the Andronikov monastery that Alexis founded. Several of the monks he trained are honored as saints themselves, among them Nikon, Savva, Andronikus, Micah, and Athanasius of Serpukhov. The elder under the oak had said the boy would lead many after him. Now he was doing it.</p>\n\n<p>The brothers saw signs they were not meant to talk about. When Prince Vladimir visited the monastery, Sergius served the Liturgy, and two monks, Isaac the Silent and Makarii, saw a fourth priest serving at the altar behind their hegumen, shining with light. No priest had come with the prince. Sergius told them that it was an angel of the Lord who served with him whenever he served, and that they must tell no one about it while he lived.</p>\n\n<p>The hardest test of his poverty came from his oldest friend in the Church. As his death drew near, Metropolitan Alexis gave Sergius a gold cross set with jewels and offered him the metropolitan see itself, the leadership of the Russian Church after him. Sergius refused. “Forgive me, master,” he said. From his youth, he told Alexis, he had not been a gold-bearer, and in his old age he wished to stay poor. The same chapter of the Life records a prophecy of Sergius about Archimandrite Michael, who later died on the way to Constantinople. Alexis died in 1378.</p>\n\n<p>Before long, a grand prince would come to the forest with a question of his own.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-letter-on-the-road\">A Letter on the Road</h2>\n\n<p>Mamai had gathered the Horde for war. Grand Prince Dmitri came to the monastery in the forest to ask the hegumen one question. Should he march?</p>\n\n<p>Sergius blessed him and, as the Life puts it, armed him with prayer. He told the prince to go out against the godless, and that he would win if God helped him. Dmitri made a vow: if he came back victorious, he would build a monastery in honor of the Mother of God.</p>\n\n<p>On the road the Russians came within sight of the Tatar host. It was so huge that they stopped, in doubt. Then a messenger arrived with a letter from Sergius.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Without any doubt, lord, go boldly against their fierceness.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— Sergius to Grand Prince Dmitri, from the Life, as quoted in A. P. Rogovich, <em>Prepodobny Sergii Radonezhsky</em>, 1922 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>The prince went forward, and the Russians won. In the Life as Pachomius revised it, Sergius knew the outcome before any messenger could bring it. At the hour of the battle, many days away, he was standing in prayer with his brothers, and he saw the fighting as if he were there. He named the men who were falling, and he prayed for them. When Dmitri came back, he visited the monastery, gave Sergius a gift, and kept his vow. Sergius built the Dormition monastery at Dubenka, and his disciple Savva became its first hegumen.</p>\n\n<p>This is the oldest form of the story. The Life never names the battlefield, gives no year, and does not name a single warrior from the monastery. A later work, the <em>Tale of the Mamai Rout</em>, places the blessing before the battle of Kulikovo Field in 1380 and brings in two monks, Peresvet and Oslyabya, who appear nowhere in the Life. The promise also grew firmer as the story was retold. The earliest text says Dmitri would win if God helped; the Life as Pachomius revised it says that with God’s help he would win and come home unharmed. By the time the historian Klyuchevsky told the story, it had become a plain prophecy:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Go boldly against the godless, without wavering, and you will win.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— Klyuchevsky’s retelling of Sergius’s blessing, quoted in A. P. Rogovich, <em>Prepodobny Sergii Radonezhsky</em>, 1922 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>Some scholars read the Life differently. The historian Kuchkin argues that the blessing belongs to an earlier battle, on the river Vozha in 1378. In every version the story keeps the same picture at its center. A prince came to a man in a patched robe and asked him what to do, and a letter reached the army just as its courage failed.</p>\n\n<p>In his own monastery, though, Sergius was waiting for a visit of another kind.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"do-not-be-afraid-chosen-one\">Do Not Be Afraid, Chosen One</h2>\n\n<p>One night Sergius prayed the akathist hymn before an icon of the Mother of God, asking her to protect his disciples and his monastery. When he finished, he turned to his disciple Micah. “Be vigilant,” he said. “We will have a wondrous visit.”</p>\n\n<p>A voice was heard: “The Most Pure comes.” A light brighter than the sun shone, and in it stood the Mother of God, with the apostles Peter and John. Sergius fell on his face. Micah lay on the ground half dead from the light. Then the Virgin touched Sergius.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Do not be afraid, my chosen one. I have come to visit you.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— The Mother of God to Sergius, in the Life of Sergius, chapter 26 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>His prayer for his disciples and his monastery had been heard, she told him. The monastery would have abundance, in his lifetime and after his death, and she would not leave this place. Afterward Sergius was too shaken to speak. When he could, he called two of the brothers, Isaac and Simon, and told them what had happened. The monastery still keeps the memory of that visit every year, on 24 August (6 September on the civil calendar).</p>\n\n<p>The monk who had once eaten moldy bread from a sieve had been promised abundance by the Mother of God. Other brothers saw signs too, and he asked each of them to keep silent. Simon, who served as ecclesiarch, the monk in charge of the church, was at the Liturgy one day when he saw fire moving across the altar. As Sergius came to receive Communion, the fire gathered itself together like a veil and went into the chalice. Sergius forbade him to tell anyone until Sergius himself was dead. Dimitry of Rostov’s later Life adds one more sign: a great flock of birds came down on the monastery, and a voice told Sergius that his disciples would be as many as the birds.</p>\n\n<p>He was an old man now, and the brothers he had gathered would soon have to go on without him.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-face-not-like-the-dead\">A Face Not Like the Dead</h2>\n\n<p>Six months before he died, Sergius handed the monastery over to his disciple Nikon and made him hegumen in his place. In September he fell ill. He called the brothers together and gave them his last instructions. They were to keep the Orthodox faith, stay of one mind with one another, keep themselves pure, welcome strangers, and avoid quarrels. And they were not to seek honors, the one thing he himself had refused all his life.</p>\n\n<p>Called by God, he told them, he was leaving them. He entrusted them to the Lord Almighty and to His Most Pure Mother, who would be their refuge and their wall against the snares of the enemy. His disciples held him up as he received Communion. Then he raised his hands and prayed, and in that prayer he died.</p>\n\n<p>It was 25 September 1392. The Life says he was seventy-eight years old. A fragrance filled the cell, and his face was bright, the brothers said, “not like a dead man’s.” He was buried at the monastery he had begun with two pairs of shoulders and a load of logs.</p>\n\n<p>Twenty-five years later, Epiphanius sat down to write his life, drawing on twenty years of gathering, and on the memories of Sergius’s own brother Stefan. His story ends at the grave. The grave had another story to tell.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"water-presses-my-body\">Water Presses My Body</h2>\n\n<p>Thirty years after his death, Sergius appeared in a dream to a devout man. Why, he asked, did they leave him so long in the grave, covered with earth? “Water presses my body.”</p>\n\n<p>Nikon, still hegumen, opened the grave on 5 July 1422, before princes and a council. Among the princes was Yury Dmitrievich, Sergius’s own godson. Water stood on both sides of the body, but it had not touched him. The body and even the robe were whole. The monks placed his relics in a shrine, and Nikon built a new church of the Trinity, this time in stone, and had it painted by Daniil and Andrei. Five years later, in 1427, a monastery was founded at Varnitsy, the village in the Rostov country that is named as his birthplace.</p>\n\n<p>The Church honored him as a saint before formal rules for canonization existed, and no single act of canonization survives. Around 1449 or 1450, Metropolitan Jona wrote of him as “prepodobny,” venerable, the title of a monk-saint, and some encyclopedias date his glorification to 1452. Not everyone agreed. In the sixteenth century the learned monk Maxim the Greek doubted that Sergius was a saint at all, because the leaders of the Church held land and wealth. It was a strange charge to bring against a man who had eaten moldy bread rather than let his monks go begging.</p>\n\n<p>For nearly five centuries the relics lay in their shrine. Then, in a new Russia, the shrine itself was opened.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-lock-of-hair-in-1919\">A Lock of Hair, 1919</h2>\n\n<p>On 1 February 1919 the new Soviet government ordered the opening of saints’ relics. On 11 April 1919 a commission came to the Trinity monastery and opened the shrine of Sergius. They found bones and a rotted robe, and in the skull a lock of fair, reddish hair, wrapped in waxed paper that looked recent. A protocol was drawn up and signed by those present, including Archimandrite Kronid, the vicar of the monastery. The filmmaker Lev Kuleshov filmed it for a newsreel.</p>\n\n<p>The Church remembers it differently. According to church tradition, before the opening, Pavel Florensky and Count Yury Olsufiev secretly took Sergius’s head from the shrine and put the skull of a Prince Trubetskoy in its place. Russian historians have also studied the account. The tradition says that the head was hidden in the altar of the Vladimirskaya church at Vinogradovo during the war, and returned to the relics in 1946.</p>\n\n<p>The shrine became an exhibit in the Lavra museum. In the summer of 1941 it was sealed and sent to Solikamsk, where it stayed from 25 July 1941 until 19 November 1944, when it came back to the Trinity cathedral.</p>\n\n<p>On Holy Saturday, 20 April 1946, the day before Easter, the relics of Sergius were handed back to the Church. They were carried from the Trinity cathedral, which still belonged to the museum, to the Dormition cathedral. Today they rest again in the Trinity cathedral.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"hegumen-of-the-russian-land\">Hegumen of the Russian Land</h2>\n\n<p>The Orthodox Church keeps two great days for Sergius. The first is 25 September, the day of his death, by the Church’s Julian calendar, which falls on 8 October on the civil calendar. On 5 July, which is 18 July on the civil calendar, she remembers the day his relics were found in 1422. He is also remembered with the saints of Radonezh, of Moscow, of Rostov and Yaroslavl, and of Vladimir. He left no writings, only his life and the words his brothers remembered.</p>\n\n<p>The stories did not end with his death. The Life in its later form lists about fifty-five miracles after it. Among them are healings at his tomb, and appearances of Sergius to the men defending his monastery during the siege of 1608 to 1610 and to Cossacks at sea.</p>\n\n<p>Russians honor him as the patron of their country, and also as the patron of students. The patron of students is the boy who could not learn his letters until an old monk under an oak gave him a piece of bread that tasted like honey.</p>\n\n<p>That boy spent his life choosing the smallest share. He gave his inheritance to his brother, split his last crust with a bear, built a porch for a sieve of moldy bread, and wore the cloth that seven others had refused. Again and again the abundance came anyway. Loaves arrived at the gate, a spring rose in the ravine, and brothers came one by one until they filled the forest and then went out to fill others. The plowman was right that he looked like an orphan. But the Mother of God had told this orphan that she would not leave his monastery, and the monastery still stands around the shrine where he lies.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"a-note-on-the-sources\">A Note on the Sources</h2>\n\n<p>The Life of Sergius was written by Epiphanius the Wise in 1417–18, but it survives only as revised by Pachomius the Serb, who worked at the Trinity monastery between about 1440 and 1459. Pachomius changed the style and added the finding of the relics and the posthumous miracles, and Simon Azarin added more miracles in the seventeenth century. Both the vision of the battle and the stronger form of the promise to Dmitri belong to the later layers of the Life. The vision of the birds is told here from Dimitry of Rostov’s Life. The Life gives no dates except the year of his death. Its age at death, seventy-eight, points to a birth in 1314, and the scholar Borisov keeps that year. The earlier line of the tradition gives an age of about seventy and a birth in 1322, which Kuchkin and Kloss accept. Nowhere does the Life name the battle before which Dmitri was blessed, and the names Kulikovo, Peresvet, and Oslyabya come from the later <em>Tale of the Mamai Rout</em>. The story of the head hidden by Florensky is church tradition, and the 1919 protocol reports the bones of the whole body. Our quotations from the Life are our own English from a modern Russian translation, and they are kept short.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"how-this-was-researched\">How This Life Was Researched</h2>\n\n<p>This life draws on four sources, in Russian and English. The main one is the fifteenth-century Life by Epiphanius and Pachomius, read in a modern Russian translation. The others are Dimitry of Rostov’s Life in an English translation, the Russian Wikipedia article with its summary of modern scholarship, and a study published in emigration in 1922. Chapters 1–13, 16, 21, 22, 25, 26, and 31–33 of the Life were read in full. Chapters 14–15, 17–20, 23–24, and 27–30, and the posthumous miracles in chapters 34–88, were not read in detail. The research flagged nine details as likely missing from popular English accounts. All translations from Russian are our own.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"sources\">Sources</h2>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Dimitry of Rostov. “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh.” Edited by John Sanidopoulos, <em>johnsanidopoulos.com</em>, Sept. 2019, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html</a>. Accessed 1 Oct. 2026.</li>\n  <li>Epiphanius the Wise, and Pachomius Logothetes. <em>Zhitie i chudesa prepodobnogo Sergiia, igumena Radonezhskogo</em> [The Life and Miracles of Our Venerable Father Sergius, Hegumen of Radonezh]. Modern Russian translation. <em>Azbyka.ru</em>, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/</a>. Accessed 1 Oct. 2026.</li>\n  <li>Rogovich, A. P. <em>Prepodobny Sergii Radonezhsky</em> [St. Sergius of Radonezh]. 1922. <em>Internet Archive</em>, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/prepodobnysergir00berl\">https://archive.org/details/prepodobnysergir00berl</a>. Accessed 1 Oct. 2026.</li>\n  <li>“Sergii Radonezhsky” [Sergius of Radonezh]. <em>Wikipedia</em>, Russian edition, <a href=\"https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9\">https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/Сергий_Радонежский</a>. Accessed 1 Oct. 2026.</li>\n</ul>\n",
