    "relic_description": "<p>Sergius died on 25 September 1392 and was buried at his Trinity monastery. On 5 July 1422 Hegumen Nikon opened the grave before princes and a council; the body and robe were reported whole, and the relics were placed in a shrine, and Nikon then built a stone Trinity church. On 11 April 1919, under a Soviet decree of 1 February 1919, a commission opened the shrine and recorded bones, a rotted robe, and a lock of fair-reddish hair wrapped in recent waxed paper in the skull; the protocol was signed by those present, including Archimandrite Kronid, and Lev Kuleshov filmed the opening. Church tradition holds that Pavel Florensky and Count Yury Olsufiev had secretly removed the head beforehand and replaced it with the skull of a Prince Trubetskoy, and that the head was hidden at Vinogradovo and rejoined the relics in 1946; the 1919 protocol reports bones of the whole body. The shrine became a museum exhibit, was evacuated to Solikamsk from 25 July 1941 to 19 November 1944, and on Holy Saturday, 20 April 1946, the relics were returned to the Church and moved to the Dormition cathedral. They now rest in the Trinity cathedral of the Lavra.</p>",
    "relic_location": "Trinity cathedral, Trinity-Sergius Lavra, Russia"
  },
  "related": {
    "miracles": [
      {
        "miracles": "<p>This list gathers the wonders attributed to St. Sergius of Radonezh in the sources we read, with the source and status of each. Sergius was venerated as a saint before the Russian Church had formal rules for canonization, so none of these accounts was examined by a Church commission. Most come from the Life written by his disciple Epiphanius the Wise about twenty-five years after his death, which survives only in the revision of Pachomius the Serb; the status line says when an account belongs to a later layer. Sergius himself tried to hide several of them, and the Life says so. The list makes no claim of scientific proof, and it does not dismiss any account.</p>\n<h2 id=\"during-his-life\">During Sergius’s Life</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m01\">Three cries before his birth, Rostov country</h3>\n<p>While his mother Maria stood at the Sunday Liturgy, the child in her womb cried out three times: at the Gospel, at the Cherubic Hymn, and at the exclamation “Holy Things for the Holy.” The women nearby asked what this child would be. Epiphanius compares the sign with those told of Ephrem the Syrian and Alypius. His brother Stefan later named the forest church for the Holy Trinity because of the three cries.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 2, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m02\">An infant who kept the fast days</h3>\n<p>As a baby, Bartholomew would not take his mother’s milk on Wednesdays and Fridays, the fast days of the Church.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 4, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m03\">The elder under the oak and the gift of reading</h3>\n<p>At seven Bartholomew could not learn his letters, and his teacher beat him in despair. Sent to find his father’s lost foals, he met a black-robed elder praying under an oak and asked for his prayers. The elder gave him a piece of blessed bread, which tasted sweet as honey; at home the boy then read the Psalter clearly in the chapel. The elder foretold that the boy would be “a dwelling of the Holy Trinity” and vanished at the gate, and the parents believed he had been an angel.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 3, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m04\">Fragrance at his tonsure</h3>\n<p>When Hegumen Mitrofan tonsured him on 7 October, the feast of Sts. Sergius and Bacchus, the young monk received Communion and the church filled with fragrance. He was twenty-three.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 6, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m05\">Demons in Lithuanian caps</h3>\n<p>While Sergius sang Matins alone in his forest church, the wall opened and demons in Lithuanian clothes and tall caps rushed in and threatened him. He drove them out with the Psalms and the sign of the cross. Some days later they attacked again in his hut, and he drove them off again.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 7, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m06\">Wolves and bears in the forest</h3>\n<p>Wolves howled around his hut and bears passed by. The Life says he feared a little, like any man, and prayed, and the animals did him no harm.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 6, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m07\">The bear at the stump</h3>\n<p>A bear came to Sergius for food, and he left bread for it on a stump or log. When there was no bread, the bear waited “like a harsh creditor wanting his debt.” When Sergius had only one piece, he broke it in two and gave the bear half, going hungry rather than cheat it. This lasted more than a year.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 7, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m08\">Fresh loaves at the gate after four days of hunger</h3>\n<p>When the monastery ran out of bread and salt, Sergius forbade the monks to beg. On the fourth day he built a porch for the old monk Daniil and was paid with a sieve of moldy bread. While the brothers grumbled and threatened to leave, someone knocked at the gate with fresh loaves. No one learned who had sent them.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry. The Life presents it as God’s providence; the donor was never found.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 10, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m09\">The spring in the ravine</h3>\n<p>When the growing community lacked water and the brothers complained, Sergius prayed in a ravine beside a puddle of rainwater, and a spring rose there. It was still in use when the Life was written. When people called it Sergius’s spring, he was angry and said the Lord, not he, had given the water.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 12, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m10\">A father’s dead child who lived</h3>\n<p>A man brought his sick infant son to Sergius, and the child died in his arms. While the father went to get a coffin, Sergius prayed on his knees, and the child lived. Sergius told the father the boy had only been cold and had warmed up in the cell, said that no one rises before the general resurrection, and forbade him to tell. The father did not keep silent.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry. Sergius himself denied that it was a miracle.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 13, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m11\">An angel serving at the Liturgy</h3>\n<p>During a visit of Prince Vladimir, the monks Isaac the Silent and Makarii saw a fourth, shining priest serving behind Sergius, though the prince had brought no priest. Sergius told them that an angel of the Lord served with him whenever he served, and that they must tell no one while he lived.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 21, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m12\">Prophecy about Archimandrite Michael</h3>\n<p>In the chapter on Metropolitan Alexis’s last days, the Life records a prophecy of Sergius about Archimandrite Michael, who later died on the way to Constantinople.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry. Recorded briefly; the notes did not record its details.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 25, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m13\">Seeing the battle against Mamai from afar</h3>\n<p>At the hour of Grand Prince Dmitri’s battle with Mamai, Sergius stood in prayer with his brothers, saw the battle from many days’ journey away, named the fallen, and prayed for them. The Life does not name the battlefield; the later <em>Tale of the Mamai Rout</em> places the blessing before Kulikovo (1380), and the historian Kuchkin links it to the battle on the Vozha (1378).</p>\n<p>Status: in the Life as revised by Pachomius (1440–59), a later layer than Epiphanius.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 22, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>; “Sergii Radonezhsky,” <a href=\"https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9\">Russian Wikipedia</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m14\">The Mother of God with Peter and John</h3>\n<p>After praying the akathist before her icon, Sergius told his disciple Micah to be vigilant, for they would have a wondrous visit. A voice announced the Most Pure, and the Mother of God appeared in a light brighter than the sun, with the apostles Peter and John. She touched Sergius, told him not to be afraid, and promised that his monastery would have abundance in his life and after, and that she would not leave the place. Micah lay half dead from the light; Sergius later told Isaac and Simon. The monastery keeps the memory on 24 August (6 September civil).</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 26, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>; “Sergii Radonezhsky,” <a href=\"https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9\">Russian Wikipedia</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m15\">Fire entering the chalice</h3>\n<p>Simon, the ecclesiarch, saw fire move about the altar during the Liturgy, then fold like a veil and enter the chalice as Sergius received Communion. Sergius forbade him to tell anyone until after his death.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 31, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m16\">A flock of birds and a promise of disciples</h3>\n<p>A great flock of birds came down on the monastery, and a voice told Sergius that his disciples would be as many.</p>\n<p>Status: told in Dimitry of Rostov’s Life (1690s compilation); not checked in the earlier Life for this list.</p>\n<p>Source: Dimitry of Rostov, “Life of Our Venerable Father Sergius of Radonezh,” English translation, <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/09/life-of-our-venerable-father-sergius-of.html\">johnsanidopoulos.com</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m17\">Fragrance and a bright face at his death, 1392</h3>\n<p>Sergius died on 25 September 1392 after receiving Communion, held up by his disciples. The cell filled with fragrance, and his face was bright, “not like a dead man’s.”</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in the earliest Life (Epiphanius, 1417–18, as kept in Pachomius’s revision). Not examined by a Church inquiry.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 32, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-his-death\">After Sergius’s Death</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m18\">A dream and an incorrupt body, 1422</h3>\n<p>Thirty years after his death, Sergius appeared in a dream to a devout man and asked why he was left so long in the grave, covered with earth: “Water presses my body.” On 5 July 1422 Hegumen Nikon opened the grave before princes, among them Sergius’s godson Yury Dmitrievich, and a council. Water stood on both sides, but the body and robe were whole and untouched by it. The relics were placed in a shrine.</p>\n<p>Status: added in Pachomius’s revision (1440–59). The date is also given by modern scholarship. The Orthodox Church keeps 5 July (18 July civil) as the feast of the finding of the relics.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, ch. 33, modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>; also “Sergii Radonezhsky,” <a href=\"https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%A1%D0%B5%D1%80%D0%B3%D0%B8%D0%B9_%D0%A0%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%B6%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%B9\">Russian Wikipedia</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m19\">Later miracles listed in the Life</h3>\n<p>The later Life lists about fifty-five posthumous miracles in chapters 34–88, among them healings at his tomb, appearances of Sergius to the men at the Trinity-Sergius monastery during the siege of 1608–10, and appearances to Cossacks at sea. Simon Azarin added miracles to the Life in the seventeenth century. These chapters were not read in detail for this list, so the accounts are not told one by one here.</p>\n<p>Status: recorded in later layers of the Life; only the chapter titles were checked.</p>\n<p>Source: Epiphanius the Wise and Pachomius Logothetes, <em>Life and Miracles of Sergius of Radonezh</em>, chs. 34–88 (chapter list), modern Russian translation, <a href=\"https://azbyka.ru/otechnik/Epifanij_Premudryj/zhitie-i-chudesa-prepodobnogo-sergija-igumena-radonezhskogo/\">Azbyka.ru</a>.</p>\n",
        "time_period": [
          "medieval_period",
          "modern_era"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "teachings": [
      {
        "teachings": "<p>Sergius left no writings. Only spiritual instructions remembered by his disciples are known, so what follows is the example of his life as his biographer Epiphanius recorded it, arranged by theme. It is the example of a life, not a body of his own doctrine. The translations from Russian are our own.</p>\n<h2 id=\"poverty-chosen-freely\">Poverty Chosen Freely</h2>\n<p>Sergius chose poverty and kept it all his life. He gave his whole inheritance to his younger brother Petr before he went into the forest. As hegumen he wore sermyaga, the coarse undyed cloth of peasants, and once took a piece of cloth that seven monks had refused and wore it until it fell apart. A visiting plowman took him for an orphan. When Metropolitan Alexis, near death, gave him a jeweled gold cross and offered him the metropolitan see, he refused.</p>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Forgive me, master: from my youth I have not been a gold-bearer.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— Sergius to Metropolitan Alexis, Life of Sergius, chapter 25 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He added that in old age he wished to stay poor. Alexis died in 1378, and Sergius remained hegumen of his forest monastery.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-with-ones-own-hands\">Work With One’s Own Hands</h2>\n<p>The hegumen of Trinity worked first and hardest. With his brother Stefan he cut logs and carried them on his shoulders to build the first church. As hegumen he was first at work and first in church, and he stood through the services without leaning on the wall. Visitors found him digging the garden. When the monastery was hungry, he took an axe and built a porch for an old monk in exchange for bread. Newcomers wore a black coat and did every kind of labor for a long time before they received the habit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-common-life\">A Common Life</h2>\n<p>At first each monk at Trinity lived in his own cell and kept his own things. Patriarch Philotheus of Constantinople sent Sergius a letter saying that one rule was missing: the monastery had no common life. With the agreement of Metropolitan Alexis, Sergius introduced a common rule, with a cellarer, cooks, bakers, and an infirmarian, and no private property for anyone. His disciples carried this life to other monasteries, among them Kirzhach, Simonov, Dubenka, Golutvin, and Vysoky.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trust-in-god-in-want\">Trust in God in Want</h2>\n<p>Sergius warned every newcomer that the forest offered hunger, thirst, and poverty, and told them to prepare their hearts for patience and many trials, not for food, drink, or rest. When the bread ran out, he forbade begging and waited. On the fourth day he worked for a sieve of moldy bread, and while the brothers grumbled, fresh loaves arrived at the gate from a donor no one ever found. He told the hungry monks to feed the men who had carried the bread first. The same trust shows in the letter he sent to Grand Prince Dmitri when the army stopped in doubt before the Tatar host:</p>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Without any doubt, lord, go boldly against their fierceness.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— Sergius to Grand Prince Dmitri, from the Life as quoted in A. P. Rogovich, 1922 (our translation)</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"hiding-gods-gifts\">Hiding God’s Gifts</h2>\n<p>Sergius did everything he could to keep praise away from himself. When people called the spring in the ravine by his name, he was angry: the Lord had given the water, he said, not he. When a dead child lived again after his prayer, he told the father the boy had only been cold, and forbade him to tell. He asked Isaac and Makarii to keep the angel at the altar secret while he lived, and Simon to say nothing of the fire in the chalice until after his death.</p>\n<h2 id=\"correction-without-anger\">Correction Without Anger</h2>\n<p>At night Sergius walked past the cells. Where he heard prayer, he blessed it; where he heard talk or laughter, he knocked on the door or window and went on. The next morning he spoke to the brothers concerned, as Epiphanius says, “from afar,” and without anger. When the monks grumbled against him over hunger and over water, he answered with patience and with prayer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"his-last-charge\">His Last Charge</h2>\n<p>When he fell ill in September 1392, Sergius gathered the brothers and told them to keep the Orthodox faith and unity among themselves, to keep themselves pure, to welcome strangers, to avoid quarrels, and not to seek honors. He entrusted them to the Lord Almighty and to His Most Pure Mother, whom he called their refuge and wall against the snares of the enemy. Six months earlier he had already handed the monastery to his disciple Nikon.</p>\n",
        "time_period": [
          "medieval_period"
        ]
      }
    ],
